WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.160
It's safe to say that Star Wars is likely
the most popular film series of all time.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.599
Not only have the official chapters and growing
number of offshoots brought in billions of

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:14.440
dollars, they've captivated hundreds of millions
the world over with classic stories of good

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.160
triumphing over evil.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.650
But it takes a lot of money, time, and effort
to put a Star Wars movie together, which means

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.289
once in awhile someone's going to blow a take.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.289
For example ...

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:25.419
Super blooper

00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:30.630
Sci-fi films naturally include dialogue with
a lot of technical words, alien planets and

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.130
races, unusual character names, and assorted
other jargon.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.930
Actors have to work hard to memorize all that
stuff, or at least be able to read it off

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.250
cue cards properly.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:43.260
But it's a fairly simple word that ended up
throwing off Mark Hamill while filming a scene

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.260
inside an X-Wing …

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.630
"Now let's get some distance before that thing
goes blah blah."

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:48.820
"Now let's get some distance before that thing
goes supernova.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.080
How do you pronounce supernova?

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.080
What's the inflection?

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.640
SUPERnova or superNOVA?."

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.710
Man vs. Sword: Round 1

00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:00.050
It would seem that the proper handling of
a lightsaber is not really among actor Liam

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.140
Neeson's, well,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.340
"...particular set of skills"

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:08.280
Anyway, while portraying Qui-Gon Jinn in The
Phantom Menace, Neeson tried to smoothly unsheathe

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.689
Star Wars' trademark weapon, but as this take
proves, he wasn't quite a real-life Jedi master...

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.860
Let's see that again — smooth!

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.200
Tongue-tied Tarkin

00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:24.820
Bloopers can add a fascinating dimension to
extremely familiar movie scenes.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:29.200
Take, for example, this well-known moment
from A New Hope, in which Princess Leia begs

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.460
and pleads with the stone-hearted Governor
Tarkin not to blow up her adopted home planet.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:38.110
Actor Peter Cushing reels around to chastise
her, but gets tongue-tied …

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.280
"No!

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:40.440
Alderaan is peaceful, we have no weapons.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.440
You can't!"

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.640
"You would prefer another target, a military
target?

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.360
Then name the ... blah!

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.280
Name it, now!

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.159
Before I go home."

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:50.640
Man vs. Sword: Round 2

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.330
At some point in the last 40 years, thousands
of kids have staged an epic, imaginary lightsaber

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:56.810
battle in their bedroom.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.619
In this blooper, Hayden Christensen looks
just like a Star Wars-loving little boy — up

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.720
to the moment he loses his balance and feels
the Force…of gravity ...

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:13.580
"You okay?"

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:14.060
"Cut!"

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.400
Stuck stormtroopers

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:20.439
Blindly serving the evil Darth Vader, stormtroopers
are intimidating, cutthroat, and unemotional

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:22.529
agents of death and destruction.

00:02:22.529 --> 00:02:24.439
What could possibly stop their march of doom?

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.800
A hole too small to fit through, apparently,
as seen in this blooper from A New Hope ...

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:39.730
Droid down

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:45.730
From the first Star Wars film until his death
in 2016, actor Kenny Baker operated R2-D2

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:46.840
from the inside.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.389
It's easy to forget that R2 is actually a
relatively clunky costume and not actually

00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:52.510
an advanced droid.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.930
This means it can tip over, or the controls
can suddenly go out, as they did in these

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:58.300
bloopers from The Phantom Menace …

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.139
Let it go

00:03:12.139 --> 00:03:16.379
Saying the right words, moving your body around
the right way, and interacting with other

00:03:16.379 --> 00:03:20.610
actors all at the same time — and in the
correct order — can be a lot to remember,

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.459
and if an actor screws up just one of those
little things, it ruins the take.

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:28.790
In this scene from Attack of the Clones, Hayden
Christensen knew he was supposed to let go

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.699
of Natalie Portman's arm — he just did it
way too early ...

00:03:31.699 --> 00:03:32.699
"Where are you going?"

00:03:32.699 --> 00:03:33.720
"To find Obi-Wan."

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.160
"No, you're not."

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.240
"Let go of me."

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.519
Obi-Whine Kenobi

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:44.980
After being offered the role of Obi-Wan Kenobi,
but before taking the part, Alec Guinness

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.760
called it "fairy-tale rubbish."

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.949
He later wrote in a letter during filming
that he didn't enjoy making the movie in part

00:03:50.949 --> 00:03:53.040
due to what he called "rubbish dialogue."

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.300
That resentment from the legendary English
actor is on display in this blooper from the

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.249
set of A New Hope, after he's upstaged by
one of Chewbacca's hairy arms ...

00:04:01.249 --> 00:04:03.960
"Here's where the fun begins."

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.740
"Well …"

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.220
Remember lines he cannot

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:16.590
Even the most vocal The Phantom Menace detractor
— of which there are many — has to admit

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:21.049
that a highlight from the first, long-awaited
Star Wars prequel was the return of Yoda.

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:25.430
Frank Oz returned to provide the Jedi Master's
grammatically wonky voice, and even though

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:29.699
Oz wasn't physically on camera at all, he
still seemed to get nervous on set ...

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:34.780
"Always two there are: no more, no less.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:37.819
Now I forgot my line!"

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.259
"Can I cut, George?"

00:04:40.259 --> 00:04:42.090
Thanks for watching!

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.890
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.249
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

